# Human-only items: what a person owes this project, never evidence and never citable by a
# claim (EV-05, GUARDRAILS 2.8, AUTONOMY.md section 3: a handoff is a ledger row, not prose).
# One line per item, four space-separated tokens:
#   <id> <disposition> evidence: none
# The id is the one the project's own records already use: an H-id of a phase verification
# record or a milestone close record (H7-1, H7-2, H8-3) or a D-id owner gate (D10, D11).
# The disposition is `handed-off` or `not-performed`, and nothing else: a handoff is never a
# done item and never blank (this file refuses the verdict vocabulary on purpose, so a row
# cannot be read as a verdict either). `handed-off` means the item has been offered to the owner with the
# procedure it needs; `not-performed` means it needs hardware or owner-local images that no
# tool on this host can supply.
# The third and fourth tokens are the literal words `evidence: none`, so a row cannot express
# an evidence tier: a person's answer of yes or no is a handoff RESULT, it is recorded here as
# `evidence: none` either way, and it never becomes a claim. A claim that wants to rest on a
# result must be raised by a separate decision, never by editing this file.
# A MISSING file means an empty set, not a missing inventory. This file is NOT read through
# `read_ids` (tools/check/lib.cmake:75-101, which appends whole lines and is only usable for
# single-column inventories), so its reader must guard on existence with `if(EXISTS ...)`,
# the way the claim profile file is guarded. This file carries no allow-empty directive,
# because emptiness is not legal for this ledger: a ledger with no rows records nothing.
# Read by the claim hook's artifact refusal (tools/check/kinds/claim.cmake, plan 10-05) and by
# later milestones, which cite a row id and open the packet in docs/handoffs.md instead of
# re-asking the owner.
# The five ids are five rows but not five human acts. H8-3 is the Phase 8 close record's id
# (08-MILESTONE-CLOSE.md section K) for the same two acts as H7-1 and H7-2 plus a look at the
# STIC pictures of a `diag-stic2-` scenario, so one act discharges both H8-3 and the two Phase
# 7 rows; a later milestone must not book a third act on the strength of this count.
# That sentence is a pointer to the five ids it was written about, not a count of this file: Phase
# 16 added three rows beside them, H16-1 (a real frontend loads the build-only shared object),
# H16-2 (an owner image's picture and its sound) and H16-3 (the word order of the owner's flat
# image). Their procedures are the packet's sections of the same names in docs/handoffs.md. The
# row count is printed by the claims step as `handoffs=<n>`, counted from this file on every run,
# so no record has to quote a figure that goes stale; like every row here the three are never a
# pass, never evidence and never a criterion of any phase.
# The D10 and D11 rows name items held in data/expected/d10-measurements.ids, cited by path
# only: that file is the inventory and this file never restates its row count.
H7-1 handed-off evidence: none
H7-2 handed-off evidence: none
H8-3 handed-off evidence: none
D10 not-performed evidence: none
D11 not-performed evidence: none
H16-1 handed-off evidence: none
H16-2 handed-off evidence: none
H16-3 handed-off evidence: none
